Suppose \(\vec x \in \mathbb{R}^3\). Let \(g(\vec x)=(x_1^2+x_2-3)^2+(x_1+x_2^2-4)^2 + x_3^2\). a) Find \(
abla g(\vec x)\). Hint: Start by finding the partial derivatives of \(g\) with respect to \(x_1\), \(x_2\), and \(x_3\). Solution Let’s start by finding the partial derivatives of \(g\) with respect to \(x_1\), \(x_2\), and \(x_3\). We’ll need to make heavy use of the (regular, scalar-to-scalar) chain rule. $$  rac{\partial g}{\partial x_1} = 2(x_1^2+x_2-3)(2x_1) + 2(x_1+x_2^2-4)(1) = 4x_1 (x_1^2+x_2-3) + 2(x_1+x_2^2-4) $$ $$  rac{\partial g}{\partial x_2} = 2(x_1^2+x_2-3) + 4x_2(x_1+x_2^2-4) \: 	ext{(notice the symmetry with the first case)} $$ $$  rac{\partial g}{\partial x_3} = 2x_3 $$ So, . $$  oxed{
abla g(\vec x) =  egin{bmatrix} 4x_1 (x_1^2+x_2-3) + 2(x_1+x_2^2-4) \\ 2(x_1^2+x_2-3) + 4x_2(x_1+x_2^2-4) \\ 2x_3 \end{bmatrix}} $$ No need to simplify the expression any further — doing so won’t make it any easier to evaluate at a specific point. b) Evaluate \(
abla g\left(  egin{bmatrix} 2 \ 1 \ 0 \end{bmatrix} ight)\). The result is a vector in \(\mathbb{R}^3\). What does it mean? . Solution $$ 
abla g(\vec x) =  egin{bmatrix} 4x_1 (x_1^2+x_2-3) + 2(x_1+x_2^2-4) \\ 2(x_1^2+x_2-3) + 4x_2(x_1+x_2^2-4) \\ 2x_3 \end{bmatrix} $$ Notice the shared terms of \(x_1^2 + x_2 - 3\) and \(x_1 + x_2^2 - 4\) in the first and second components. To make the computation of the gradient at \( egin{bmatrix} 2 \ 1 \ 0 \end{bmatrix}\) easier, let’s compute these values first. $$ x_1^2 + x_2 - 3 = 2^2 + 1 - 3 = 2, \quad x_1 + x_2^2 - 4 = 2 + 1^2 - 4 = -1 $$ Then, . $$  oxed{
abla g\left(  egin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} ight) =  egin{bmatrix} 4x_1(x_1^2+x_2-3) + 2(x_1+x_2^2-4) \\ 2(x_1^2+x_2-3) + 4x_2(x_1+x_2^2-4) \\ 2x_3 \end{bmatrix} =  egin{bmatrix} 4(2)(2) + 2(-1) \\ 2(2) + 4(1)(-1) \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} =  egin{bmatrix} 14 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}} $$ The vector \( egin{bmatrix} 14 \ 0 \ 0 \end{bmatrix}\) describes the direction of steepest ascent at the point \( egin{bmatrix} 2 \ 1 \ 0 \end{bmatrix}\). c) Why is it guaranteed that \(g(\vec x)\) has a global minimum? . Solution \(g(\vec x) = (x_1^2 + x_2 - 3)^2 + (x_1 + x_2^2 - 4)^2 + x_3^2\) is a sum of three squares, each of which is \(\geq 0\). So, knowing nothing else about what is being squared, we know that \(g(\vec x) \geq 0\) for all \(\vec x\), and so \(g(\vec x)\) has a global minimum of something, whether it’s 0 or some positive number.

