Soon, we will start to learn about matrices. In this problem, we’ll start to connect what we’ve learned about vectors and spans to matrices. In this question, we’ll consider the matrix \(A\): . $$ A =  egin{bmatrix} 5 &amp; 3 &amp; 5 &amp; 2 \\ 3 &amp; 0 &amp; -6 &amp; 4 \\ -2 &amp; 0 &amp; 4 &amp; 3 \\ 8 &amp; 2 &amp; -6 &amp; -8 \\ 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 3 &amp; 0 \end{bmatrix} $$ \(A\) has 5 rows and 4 columns. There are two ways of looking at \(A\): . | As a collection of 4 “column” vectors, each in \(\mathbb{R}^5\), stacked side-by-side. | As a collection of 5 “row” vectors, each in \(\mathbb{R}^4\), stacked on top of each other. | . a) (3 pts) Using the algorithm in Chapter 4.2, find a linearly independent set of vectors in \(\mathbb{R}^5\) with the same span as the column vectors of \(A\). How many vectors are in this set? . Solution Our goal is to find the set of linearly independent column vectors for the matrix \(A\), as is outlined in the notes. Let . $$  egin{align*} \vec{v_1}= egin{bmatrix} 5 \\ 3 \\ -2 \\ 8 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \qquad \vec{v_2}=  egin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \qquad \vec{v_3} =  egin{bmatrix} 5 \\ -6 \\ 4 \\ -6 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} \qquad \vec{v_4} =  egin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 3 \\ -8 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \end{align*} $$ \(i=2,S=\lbrace\vec{v_1}brace: \vec{v_2} 
otin 	ext{span}(S)\). There’s no way to make \(\vec{v_2}\) as a linear combination of \(v_1\) because of the 0’s in \(\vec{v_2}\). So, we add \(\vec{v_2}\) to the set. \(i=3,S=\lbrace\vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}brace:\) Let’s try to write \(\vec{v_3}\) as a linear combination of \(\vec{v_1}\) and \(\vec{v_2}\), i.e. solve the system of equations we get from \(a\vec{v_1} + b\vec{v_2} = \vec{v_3}\): . $$  egin{align} 5a + 3b &amp;= 5 \\ 3a &amp;= -6 \\ -2a &amp;= 4 \\ 8a + 2b &amp;= -6 \\ a + b &amp;= 3 \end{align} $$ Equations (2) and (3) tell us \(a = -2\). Plugging this into equations (1), (4), and (5) each tell us that \(b = 5\). So, \(a = -2, b = 5\) satisfy all 5 equations, meaning that \(\vec{v_3}\) is a linear combination of \(\vec{v_1}\) and \(\vec{v_2}\). (If we found that some equations implied \(b = -5\) and some implied \(b\) was something else, the equations would be inconsistent, meaning that \(\vec{v_3}\) is not a linear combination of \(\vec{v_1}\) and \(\vec{v_2}\).) . Since \(\vec{v_3} \in 	ext{span}(S)\), we don’t add it to \(S\). \(i=4,S=\lbrace\vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}brace:\) Let’s try to write \(\vec{v_4}\) as a linear combination of the vectors in \(S\), i.e. solve the system of equations we get from \(a\vec{v_1}+b\vec{v_2}=\vec{v_4}\): . $$  egin{align} 5a + 3b &amp;= 2 	ag{1} \\ 3a &amp;= 4 	ag{2} \\ -2a &amp;= 3 	ag{3} \\ 8a + 2b &amp;= -9 	ag{4} \\ a + b &amp;= 0 	ag{5} \end{align} $$ In equations (2) and (3), simplifying for \(a\) gives us \(a= rac{4}{3}\) and \(a=- rac{3}{2}\). This is a contradiction, so \(\vec{v_4} 
otin 	ext{span}(S)\), so we should add it to the set. Therefore, our set of linearly independent vectors in \(\mathbb{R}^5\) is \( oxed{S=\lbrace\vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, \vec{v_4}brace}\), a set of 3 vectors. b) (4 pts) Find a linearly independent set of vectors in \(\mathbb{R}^4\) with the same span as the row vectors of \(A\). How many vectors are in this set? . Solution We can use a similar process as we did in the previous part, but on the rows of \(A\) rather than the columns. Let . $$  egin{align*} \vec{v_1}= egin{bmatrix} 5 \\ 3 \\ 5 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} \qquad \vec{v_2}=  egin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \\ -6 \\ 4\end{bmatrix} \qquad \vec{v_3} =  egin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 0 \\ 4 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} \qquad \vec{v_4} =  egin{bmatrix} 8 \\ 2 \\ -6 \\ -8 \end{bmatrix} \qquad \vec{v_5}= egin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1\\ 3 \\0\end{bmatrix} \end{align*} $$ \(i=2,S=\lbrace\vec{v_1}brace: \vec{v_2} 
otin 	ext{span}(S)\). There’s no way to make \(\vec{v_2}\) as a linear combination of \(v_1\) because of the 0 in \(\vec{v_2}\). So, we add \(\vec{v_2}\) to the set. \(i=3,S=\lbrace\vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}brace:\) Let’s try to write \(\vec v_3\) as a linear combination of \(\vec{v_1}\) and \(\vec{v_2}\), i.e. solve the system of equations we get from \(a\vec{v_1} + b\vec{v_2} = \vec{v_3}\): . $$  egin{align} 5a+3b&amp;=-2 	ag{1} \\ 3a &amp;= 0 	ag{2} \\ 5a-6b &amp;= 4 	ag{3} \\ 2a - 4b &amp;= 3 	ag{4} \end{align} $$ In equation (2), we see that \(a=0\). Plugging this into equations (1), (3), and (4) gives us: . $$  egin{align*} 3b &amp;= -2 \\ 5b &amp;= 4 \\ -4b &amp;= 3 \end{align*} $$ These give us contradictions, so \(\vec{v_3} 
otin 	ext{span}(S)\), so we should add it to the set. \(i=4,S=\lbrace\vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, \vec{v_3}brace:\) Let’s try to write \(\vec{v_4}\) as a linear combination of the vectors in \(S\), i.e. solve the system of equations we get from \(a\vec{v_1}+b\vec{v_2}+c\vec{v_3}=\vec{v_4}\): . $$  egin{align} 5a+3b -2c&amp;=8 	ag{1} \\ 3a &amp;= 2 	ag{2} \\ 5a-6b +4c&amp;= -6 	ag{3} \\ 2a - 4b +3c&amp;= -8 	ag{4} \end{align} $$ In equation (2), we see that \(a= rac{2}{3}\). Plugging this into equations (1), (3), and (4) gives us: . $$  egin{align*}  rac{10}{3} + 3b -2c &amp;= 8 \implies 3b -2c =  rac{14}{3} \\  rac{10}{3} -6b +4c &amp;= -6 \implies -6b +4c = - rac{28}{3} \\  rac{4}{3} -4b +3c &amp;= -8 \implies -4b +3c = - rac{28}{3} \end{align*} $$ Notice that the new first and second equations are the same; the second is just the first multiplied by -2. Since the second and third equations don’t look like parallel lines, they will intersect somewhere, and so there does exist a solution for \(a, b, c\). We don’t even need to bother looking for it, though you can verify yourself that \(a =  rac{2}{3}, b = - rac{14}{3}, c = - rac{28}{3}\) satisfy all four original equations. So, \(\vec{v_4} \in 	ext{span}(S)\), so we don’t add it to the set. \(i = 5, S = \lbrace\vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, \vec{v_3}brace:\) Let’s try to write \(\vec{v_5}\) as a linear combination of the vectors in \(S\), i.e. solve the system of equations we get from \(a\vec{v_1}+b\vec{v_2}+c\vec{v_3}=\vec{v_5}\): . $$  egin{align} 5a+3b -2c&amp;=1 	ag{1} \\ 3a &amp;= 1 	ag{2} \\ 5a-6b +4c&amp;= 3 	ag{3} \\ 2a - 4b +3c&amp;= 0 	ag{4} \end{align} $$ You can verify yourself that \(a =  rac{1}{3}, b = - rac{10}{3}, c = - rac{14}{3}\) satisfy all four equations. So, \(\vec{v_5} \in 	ext{span}(S)\), so we don’t add it to the set. Therefore, our set of linearly independent vectors in \(\mathbb{R}^4\) is \( oxed{S=\lbrace\vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, \vec{v_3}brace}\), a set of 3 vectors. You should have found that the number of vectors you found in both parts is the same. This is not a coincidence, it is true for any matrix — the number of linearly independent columns is the same as the number of linearly independent rows. This number is called the rank of the matrix. If you were to run the following Python code, the number you’d see back is the number of linearly independent vectors you found in both parts. import numpy as np A = np.array([[5, 3, 5, 2], [3, 0, -6, 4], [-2, 0, 4, 3], [8, 2, -6, -8], [1, 1, 3, 0]]) np.linalg.matrix_rank(A) . c) (3 pts) Open the the supplemental Jupyter Notebook we’ve created for Homework 4, which can either be found here on DataHub, or here in the course GitHub repository. Complete the three tasks within orange lines, related to the introduction we’ve provided above. Include screenshots of your code and its output as part of your PDF. Solution . d) (2 pts) Using what you observed in the notebook, by hand (that is, without using Python), compute the result of the following matrix-vector multiplication: . $$  egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 4 &amp; 5 \\ 3 &amp; 9 &amp; 8 \\ 4 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} $$ Solution As we saw, the product will contain the dot product of the rows of the matrix with the vector. So, we can compute each dot product manually: . $$  egin{align*} 	ext{first row of output:} \quad 2 \cdot 4 + 4 \cdot 1 + 5 \cdot 3 &amp;= 8 + 4 + 15 = 27 \\ 	ext{second row of output:} \quad 3 \cdot 4 + 9 \cdot 1 + 8 \cdot 3 &amp;= 12 + 9 + 24 = 45 \\ 	ext{third row of output:} \quad 4 \cdot 4 + 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 3 &amp;= 16 + 0 + 3 = 19 \end{align*} $$ Therefore, the product is \( oxed{ egin{bmatrix} 27 \ 45 \ 19 \end{bmatrix}}\).

